        SparringArc(
            personaId: "t1-champion-vp-eng",
            botRating: 1550,
            openingBuyerLine: "Hey — full disclosure, my staff engineer forwarded me your launch post with the subject line 'PLEASE.' Caps his, not mine. So the door's already open.",
            startNode: "n1",
            nodes: [
                "n1": SparringNode(buyerLine: "Our CI situation is genuinely embarrassing — forty-minute builds, flaky tests, the whole cliché. Honestly, just demo me. I'm a see-it-then-believe-it person.", candidates: [
                    SparringCandidate(text: "Love that energy — screen coming up now. Watch the build graph on the left: most teams see runtimes drop by half inside the first week.", evalDelta: -0.5, techniqueTags: ["gain-framing", "social-proof"], next: "n2b", rationale: "He asked, so it feels like service — but a demo aimed at a problem you haven't sized lands as a screensaver. You just spent the discovery window proving features to a man who hasn't said what broken costs."),
                    SparringCandidate(text: "Deal — you'll get the demo. First though: 'genuinely embarrassing' is a strong phrase for build times. What happened?", evalDelta: 0.4, techniqueTags: ["mirroring", "calibrated-question"], next: "n2a", rationale: "Echo the emotion, then open the door and get out of the way. 'Embarrassing' is not an engineering metric — something happened, and whatever it is beats any demo you could give."),
                    SparringCandidate(text: "Happy to — quick context first so I show the right things: how big is the eng org, and what does the stack look like?", evalDelta: 0.1, techniqueTags: ["calibrated-question", "concrete-construal"], next: "n2a", rationale: "A Situation question, and a cheap one — org size and stack were in his job post. You bought a turn of setup with a question Google could have answered."),
                ]),
                "n2a": SparringNode(buyerLine: "Two weeks ago a release slipped three days because the deploy pipeline flaked twice in a row, and nobody could tell me if it was the tests or the infra. That's the flavor. So — demo? I want to see the flaky-test thing.", candidates: [
                    SparringCandidate(text: "Before the demo — a three-day slip, and nobody could say why. Where did those three days land? Who felt them besides you?", evalDelta: 0.4, techniqueTags: ["spin-implication", "multi-threading"], next: "n3", rationale: "This is where most reps stop — they get a Problem answer and reach for the demo. The three days is the problem; where the three days LANDED is the deal. Amplify before you show anything."),
                    SparringCandidate(text: "Perfect segue — the flake detector is our best screen. Let me show you exactly how it would have caught that deploy before it burned you.", evalDelta: -0.5, techniqueTags: ["concrete-construal", "gain-framing"], next: "n3", rationale: "It maps his story straight onto your feature, which is exactly why it's tempting — and it caps the pain at 'a flaky deploy' when he was one question from telling you what the slip cost. The gap stays your size instead of his."),
                    SparringCandidate(text: "That tracks — we hear the tests-or-infra ambiguity constantly. Is it mostly deploys, or does it show up across the whole pipeline?", evalDelta: 0.1, techniqueTags: ["social-proof", "calibrated-question"], next: "n3", rationale: "Breadth when the moment asked for depth. Another Problem question buys another Problem answer — you're collecting symptoms sideways instead of following the one that costs money down."),
                ]),
                "n2b": SparringNode(buyerLine: "Okay, that was slick — the flake screen especially. Tell you what: send the deck and ballpark pricing, I'll float it upstairs and we'll see where it goes.", candidates: [
                    SparringCandidate(text: "Deck and pricing tonight, plus a one-pager on the ROI — teams your size usually make the math work inside a quarter, which upstairs tends to love.", evalDelta: -0.4, techniqueTags: ["social-proof", "gain-framing"], next: "n3", rationale: "The deck is how warm calls die. He floats your slides, the CTO asks one hard question, he doesn't have the answer, and the whole thing quietly becomes 'we looked at a tool once.'"),
                    SparringCandidate(text: "Will do. Before I send anything — is 'upstairs' the CTO, and is this his budget or yours?", evalDelta: 0.1, techniqueTags: ["calibrated-question", "multi-threading"], next: "n3", rationale: "A real qualification question, so it isn't wasted — but it accepts the deck-and-float plan whole. You're asking who owns the budget for a pitch neither of you has built yet."),
                    SparringCandidate(text: "I'll be straight: I demoed before understanding your setup. Backwards. When this goes upstairs, what's the first question you can't answer yet?", evalDelta: 0.5, techniqueTags: ["labeling", "calibrated-question"], next: "n3", rationale: "Owning the backwards order buys back the room, and the question does the real work: 'what can't you answer yet' turns a deck request into the exact list of things discovery should have found."),
                ]),
                "n3": SparringNode(buyerLine: "Look, between us? The CTO ran a velocity review last month and my org's line was the flat one. In front of the other VPs. So when the team whines about CI, it's not really about CI — if you follow me.", candidates: [
                    SparringCandidate(text: "I follow. How's your standing with the CTO otherwise — is he a numbers guy or a narrative guy when it comes to reviews like that?", evalDelta: 0.0, techniqueTags: ["calibrated-question"], next: "n4", rationale: "Useful color, wrong altitude. You stepped past the emotion he just showed you to gather stakeholder intel — the intel keeps; the moment doesn't."),
                    SparringCandidate(text: "The flat line, in front of the other VPs. So this isn't a tooling purchase — it's what the next review looks like.", evalDelta: 0.5, techniqueTags: ["labeling", "concrete-construal"], next: "n4", rationale: "He handed you the real problem in a whisper and you named it back without flinching. The label costs nothing, proves you heard the subtext, and moves the deal onto the axis it was always on."),
                    SparringCandidate(text: "That's exactly the story our velocity dashboard tells — before-and-after charts your CTO can read at a glance. Teams have used them in their own board reviews.", evalDelta: -0.4, techniqueTags: ["social-proof", "gain-framing"], next: "n4", rationale: "He confided; you pitched. Two well-evidenced techniques aimed at the one moment that called for neither — the confession needed an acknowledgment and it got a dashboard."),
                ]),
                "n4": SparringNode(buyerLine: "Fine, you want numbers: thirty engineers, call it five, six hours a week each lost to CI roulette. Which — saying it out loud — is horrifying. Okay, you've earned it: Thursday, demo, my whole team. They'll love the flake screen. Yes?", candidates: [
                    SparringCandidate(text: "Yes to Thursday. Who's in the room — and is anyone there the type to say 'we could build this ourselves'?", evalDelta: 0.1, techniqueTags: ["multi-threading", "calibrated-question"], next: "n5b", rationale: "Smart logistics — scouting the build-it-ourselves voice especially. But it books the demo without banking the number; you're preparing for the room instead of finishing the math that fills it."),
                    SparringCandidate(text: "Thursday works — I'll bring the full flake-detection walkthrough and load your repos into a sandbox so it feels real from minute one.", evalDelta: -0.5, techniqueTags: ["concrete-construal", "assumptive"], next: "n5b", rationale: "It feels like the deal advancing — team demo, real repos, momentum. But he just said 'horrifying' and you changed the subject to Thursday. The number was one turn from becoming his business case; now it's a calendar invite."),
                    SparringCandidate(text: "Thursday's yours. Sit with the math one second first — that's a hundred fifty engineer-hours a week. If it went to near zero, what changes at the next velocity review?", evalDelta: 0.4, techniqueTags: ["spin-implication", "spin-need-payoff"], next: "n5a", rationale: "Take the demo off the table as a fight, then make the horror specific and point it at the review he actually cares about. The buyer who answers this question is writing his own pitch."),
                ]),
                "n5a": SparringNode(buyerLine: "Honestly? If CI stopped being the excuse... I'd finally find out whether my org is slow or just blocked. Which is either the best thing you could sell me or the worst. Depends what the chart says after.", candidates: [
                    SparringCandidate(text: "That's the realest thing anyone's said to me this week. Say more about the 'worst' half.", evalDelta: 0.5, techniqueTags: ["labeling", "mirroring"], next: "n6", rationale: "He just told you this purchase has stakes bigger than tooling, and fear that close to the surface only comes out once. Hold it open — whatever he says next is the real decision criterion."),
                    SparringCandidate(text: "Fair. Either way, wouldn't you rather know? That's kind of the point of instrumenting it in the first place.", evalDelta: 0.1, techniqueTags: ["reverse-objection"], next: "n6", rationale: "True, and a little glib. 'Wouldn't you rather know' flips his fear into a slogan — he'll nod, and the thing underneath goes back underground with the nod."),
                    SparringCandidate(text: "The chart says blocked, in my experience — nine times out of ten the org is fine and the pipeline's the villain. Our before-and-afters back that up.", evalDelta: -0.4, techniqueTags: ["social-proof", "gain-framing"], next: "n6", rationale: "Reassurance feels kind and closes the door. You answered the question he's afraid of instead of letting him weigh it — and comfort he didn't earn from his own numbers won't survive the CTO asking it colder."),
                ]),
                "n5b": SparringNode(buyerLine: "Locked for Thursday. One heads-up — my infra lead is convinced we should build this in-house with Buildkite and a couple of scripts. He'll be in the room, and he argues like a lawyer.", candidates: [
                    SparringCandidate(text: "Good — skeptics make the best demos. I'll bring the build-vs-buy TCO deck; the maintenance math alone usually ends that conversation in about four slides.", evalDelta: -0.5, techniqueTags: ["authority", "contrast"], next: "n6", rationale: "Walking into a lawyer's courtroom holding the vendor's spreadsheet. The TCO deck is your math in your favor — he'll shred the assumptions live, and the whole team watches the champion's tool lose a fair fight."),
                    SparringCandidate(text: "What's his case? Genuinely — walk me through the build-it version the way he'd pitch it. If it holds up Thursday, I'd rather hear it today.", evalDelta: 0.4, techniqueTags: ["calibrated-question", "accusation-audit"], next: "n6", rationale: "Inviting the strongest objection early is the only way to own it. Getting the infra lead's pitch from the champion's mouth today beats debating the lawyer live on Thursday."),
                    SparringCandidate(text: "Noted. Worst case, Buildkite-plus-scripts and us aren't mutually exclusive — plenty of teams run both for a quarter and let the results argue it out.", evalDelta: 0.1, techniqueTags: ["social-proof", "concrete-construal"], next: "n6", rationale: "Reasonable and low-heat, but it concedes the frame that this is a tooling bake-off. The build-vs-buy fight is a proxy war; the real ground is the velocity review, and nobody's fighting there yet."),
                ]),
                "n6": SparringNode(buyerLine: "One more thing, and it stays in this call: I got pulled into Series B prep on Monday. New spend is frozen until the round closes — could be one quarter, could be two. I like this. I can't buy it. Still want Thursday?", candidates: [
                    SparringCandidate(text: "Keep Thursday, lose the order form — the freeze changes the paperwork, not the problem. When it lifts, what has to be on one page for you to be first through the door? Your numbers, not mine.", evalDelta: 0.5, techniqueTags: ["mutual-close-plan", "spin-need-payoff"], next: "n7", rationale: "A freeze kills deals that live on momentum and feeds deals that live on math. Build the one-pager in his numbers now and the day the round closes, the champion walks in armed — a calendar without a push."),
                    SparringCandidate(text: "Between us then — if we paper it this week it books as pre-freeze spend. I can hold current pricing through Friday, and honestly nobody upstairs would blink.", evalDelta: -0.6, techniqueTags: ["scarcity", "loss-framing"], next: "n7", rationale: "You just asked a VP to smuggle a line item past his own CFO during a raise. Even if he wanted to, he can't — and the ask alone recategorizes you from 'person building my case' to 'vendor hitting a quarter.'"),
                    SparringCandidate(text: "Understood — raises eat everything. Let's park Thursday, and I'll check back in once the round closes. Congrats on the B, by the way.", evalDelta: 0.0, techniqueTags: ["takeaway", "liking"], next: "n7", rationale: "Graceful, respectful, and it hands the deal to entropy. 'Check back after the round' is where warm deals go to be forgotten — the freeze stopped the purchase, not the conversation, and you conceded both."),
                ], isSpike: true),
                "n7": SparringNode(buyerLine: "Okay, you keep not selling me, which is either very good or very sneaky. Real question: the day the freeze lifts I get maybe ten minutes with the CTO. What am I saying? Write my pitch.", candidates: [
                    SparringCandidate(text: "Easy: 'CI costs us a headcount and a half of engineering time; this cuts build times in half in week one per their benchmarks; it pays for itself inside a quarter.' Clean, CFO-proof.", evalDelta: -0.4, techniqueTags: ["gain-framing", "precise-anchor"], next: "n8", rationale: "A tight pitch, and it's yours. The first hard question — 'whose benchmarks?' — knocks it over, because he's holding borrowed words. Reps write pitches; champions grow them. He asked you to do his push-ups."),
                    SparringCandidate(text: "Here's what I heard today: thirty engineers, six hours a week, a flat line in the velocity review, and an infra lead itching to build. Your pitch lives somewhere in that list.", evalDelta: 0.1, techniqueTags: ["summary-close", "concrete-construal"], next: "n8", rationale: "A faithful recap, and closer than the deck — but it's still your voice doing the assembling. You laid the parts on the table; the winning move is making him pick them up."),
                    SparringCandidate(text: "You've had the pitch for half an hour — I've just been the one taking notes. Give me the ten-minute version right now, out loud, like I'm the CTO on a bad day. Where does it start?", evalDelta: 0.4, techniqueTags: ["spin-need-payoff", "commitment-consistency"], next: "n8", rationale: "A pitch he assembles out loud is one he can defend under interruption — that's the entire difference between a champion and a forwarder. Your notes fill gaps afterward; the words have to start as his."),
                ]),
                "n8": SparringNode(buyerLine: "Fine. 'We're burning a headcount and a half on CI roulette — that's my flat line in the velocity review, that's my miss. Fixing it is one line of tooling spend, and the chart moves before the next board deck.' ...Huh. That did not feel like your pitch.", candidates: [
                    SparringCandidate(text: "Told you it was yours. I'll send a recap of everything tonight, and let's loosely aim to reconnect once the round's done — you've got my number.", evalDelta: 0.1, techniqueTags: ["summary-close", "liking"], next: "end", rationale: "Friendly, honest, unstructured — 'loosely aim' is how thawed budgets get spent on whoever showed up with a date. He built the case; it deserved a calendar, not a vibe."),
                    SparringCandidate(text: "Because it isn't mine. Here's what I'd like to do: I write that up tonight as your one-pager, verbatim, and we put thirty minutes on the calendar for the week the round closes. Thursday's demo becomes act two.", evalDelta: 0.5, techniqueTags: ["mutual-close-plan", "concrete-construal"], next: "end", rationale: "The close available here was never a signature — it was the pitch in his voice, papered, with a date on the thaw. Verbatim matters: the moment your vocabulary creeps in, it stops surviving the CTO's first question."),
                    SparringCandidate(text: "That's the pitch. Now let's make it inevitable — I'll draft the order form at current pricing with a freeze-contingent start date, so the day the round closes there's nothing left to decide.", evalDelta: -0.6, techniqueTags: ["assumptive", "loss-framing"], next: "end", rationale: "Eight turns of patience, spent in one sentence. Paper during a freeze isn't momentum, it's evidence — the thing a nervous VP can get caught holding. He gave you his pitch; you asked for his signature anyway."),
